    "title": "Duke women's basketball falls to UCLA in NCAA Tournament Elite Eight",
    "text": "Updated March 29, 2026, 6:51 p.m. ETNo. 3-seeded Duke women's basketball was stopped from advancing to the Final Four for the second straight year, this time by UCLA. The Blue Devils lost 70-58 on Sunday, March 29.The game started in Duke's favor, with Taina Mair dropping 12 points and Riley Nelson 11 in the first half. But the Blue Devils couldn't hold on after the break and were outscored 39-17 in the second half by the Bruins.Mair led with 21 points, 7 rebounds and 6 assists, while Ashlon Jackson, who scored the game-winner against LSU on Friday, didn't score but dished out five assists. Nelson added 13 points, followed by Delaney Thomas with 11 and Toby Fournier with 10.The revenge tour is officially over for the Blue Devils.The last time Duke made a Final Four was 2006.See what happened between No. 3 Duke-No. 1 UCLA women's basketball in the Elite Eight of the NCAA Women's Tournament:Buy Duke women's basketball ticketsDuke vs UCLA women's basketball live updatesTEAM1Q2Q3Q4QFINALDuke211881158UCLA1714201970 5:17 p.m. | Duke falls short of Final Four for second-straight yearThe Bruins came back in the second half and dominated, blocking the Blue Devils from making the Final Four for a second-straight year. UCLA won the Sacramento Regional and heads to Phoenix off a 70-58 victory.Taina Mair had 21 points, 7 rebounds and 6 assists while Riley Nelson had 13 points and 3 rebounds. Delaney Thomas has 12 points, 6 rebounds and 2 assists while Toby Fournier had 10 points, 4 rebounds and 1 assist and Ashlon Jackson added 2 rebounds and 5 assists. Of 58 points, only 2 came from off the bench for the Blue Devils, the rest were collected by four of the starting five members. 4:43 p.m. | UCLA pulls ahead at the end of the third quarterThe Bruins have taken the lead over the Blue Devils for the first time since just 26 seconds into the game, going up 51-47 and outscoring Duke 20-8. Taina Mair has 16 points, 5 rebounds and 4 assists while Riley Nelson has 16 points, 3 rebounds and 1 assist. Delaney Thomas has 5 rebounds and Ashlon Jackson, after scoring the game winner against LSU, has 0 points but 5 assists. 4:06 p.m. | UCLA finishes on 4-for-5 from the field, but still trails at halftimeThe Blue Devils lead the Bruins 39-31 at the end of the second quarter. Taina Mair has 12 points, 2 rebounds, 3 assists and 2 steal while Riley Nelson adds 11 points, 1 rebound and 1 assist. Toby Fournier has 8 points while Ashlon Jackson has 4 assists. 3:39 p.m. | Duke leads UCLA at the end of the first quarterThe Blue Devils lead the Bruins 21-17 at the end of the first quarter. Taina Mair has 8 points, 1 assist and 2 steals while Riley Nelson adds 7 points, 1 rebound and 1 assist.Duke vs UCLA women's basketball startersThis section will be updated when the information becomes available.Duke: Ashlon Jackson (G), Riley Nelson (G), Taina Mair (G), Delaney Thomas (F), Toby Fournier (F).UCLA: Kiki Rice (G), Charlisse Leger-Walker (G), Gianna Kneepkens (G), Gabriela Jaquez (F), Lauren Betts (C).What channel is Duke vs UCLA women's basketball on?TV channel: ABCStreaming: ESPN Unlimited, FuboDuke vs. UCLA women's basketball is being broadcast on ABC and will be available for streaming via ESPN Unlimited or Fubo, which offers a free trial for new subscribers.Duke vs UCLA women's basketball start timeDate: Sunday, March 29Time: 3 p.m. ETDuke vs. UCLA women's basketball tips off at 3 p.m. ET from Golden 1 Center in Sacramento, Calif., on Sunday, March 29.Duke vs UCLA women's basketball historySeries record: Tied, 2-2Duke's last win: Nov. 10, 2000; 80-52UCLA's last win: Nov. 27, 2025; 89-59Duke women's basketball schedule 2025-26Record: 27-8 overall, 16-2 ACCNovember (3-5)Nov. 3: Baylor 58, Duke 52Nov. 9: Duke 91, Holy Cross 48Nov. 12: Duke 83, Norfolk State 32Nov. 14: West Virginia 57, Duke 49Nov. 16: Duke 71, Liberty 57Nov. 20: South Florida 85, Duke 72Nov. 26: South Carolina 83, Duke 66Nov. 27: UCLA 89, Duke 59December (4-1)Dec. 4: LSU 93, Duke 77Dec. 7: Duke 70, Virginia Tech 54Dec. 18: Duke 97, South Dakota State 54Dec. 20: Duke 76, Belmont 46Dec. 28: Duke 71, Syracuse 51January (8-0)Jan. 1: Duke 100, Boston College 49Jan. 4: Duke 82, Notre Dame 68Jan. 8: Duke 78, Cal 74Jan. 11: Duke 67, Stanford 60Jan. 15: Duke 65, Virginia 58Jan. 18: Duke 93, Georgia Tech 46Jan. 24: Duke 95, Pitt 41Jan. 29: Duke 74, Miami 58February (6-1)Feb. 1: Duke 80, Wake Forest 44Feb. 5: Duke 59, Louisville 58Feb. 8: Duke 95, SMU 36Feb. 15: Duke 72, UNC 68Feb. 19: Duke 83, NC State 65Feb. 22: Clemson 53, Duke 51Feb. 26: Duke 80, Florida State 52March (6-1)March 1: UNC 74, Duke 69March 6: Duke 60, Clemson 46March 7: Duke 65, Notre Dame 63March 8: Duke 70, Louisville 65 (OT)March 20: Duke 81, Charleston 64March 22: Duke 69, Baylor 46March 27: Duke 87, LSU 85March 29: vs. UCLA, 3 p.m. ET (ESPN)AprilApril 3: NCAA Women's Final Four — Phoenix, Ariz.April 5: NCAA Women's Championship — Phoenix, Ariz.",
